> powerseries exp(t)
power series of e^t about t = 0
  Taylor coefficients c_0..c_7: 1, 1, 1/2, 1/6, 1/24, 1/120, 1/720, 0.0001984126984
  = sum_{m>=0} c_m t^m, c_m = 1/m!
  matches e^u: radius of convergence inf
> powerseries sin(t)
power series of sin(t) about t = 0
  Taylor coefficients c_0..c_7: 0, 1, 0, -1/6, 0, 1/120, 0, -0.0001984126984
  = sum_{k>=0} (-1)^k/(2k + 1)! * t^(2k + 1)
  radius of convergence inf
> powerseries 1/(1-t)
power series of 1/(1 - t) about t = 0
  = sum_{n>=0} a_n t^n with a_n = 1
  radius of convergence 1 (the nearest pole)
  check: the series at t = 1/3 sums to 1.5, f = 1.5
> taylor sin(x) x at 0 order 9
Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of sin(x) at x = 0, through order 9:
  sin(x) = x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 - x^7/5040 + x^9/362880 + O(x^10)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 0 1 0 -1/6 0 1/120 0 -1/5040 0 1/362880
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 2.51e-19 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 3.39e-21 at ± 1/20, 0 at ± 1/40 (at rounding level)
> radius x^n/n!
power series sum(n, 0, inf, x^n/n!) in x
  center x = 0; coefficients a_n = 1/n! of x^(n)
  radius of convergence R = inf (ratio test: |a_(n+1)/a_n| -> 0)
  interval of convergence: every x (absolutely)
  sum = e^x    for |x| < inf (e^u)
